      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "Development of the coronary pressure wire has facilitated the measurement of fractional flow reserve (FFR) to assess the functional severity of coronary artery stenoses."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "This study evaluated the correlations between FFR and myocardial direct counts of technetium-99m(99mTc)-sestamibi in 20 patients (16 men, 4 women, mean age 66 +/- 8 years) who underwent 99mTc-sestamibi single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) with the 2-day protocol using 740 MBq of 99mTc-sestamibi each day. Visual assessment of myocardial imaging and quantitative analysis with the measurement of percent uptake and direct count of 99mTc-sestamibi were performed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Visual assessment of myocardial imaging revealed that reversibility of 99mTc-sestamibi perfusion defects was correlated with FFR of < 0.75, which is regarded as functionally important stenosis (17/20 vs 3/20, kappa = 0.71, p < 0.002). Regional reversibility score did not correlate with FFR (r = -0.40, p = NS). Quantitative analysis revealed that the change in 99mTc-sestamibi percent uptake with pharmacologic stress using adenosine triphosphate disodium (ATP) also did not correlate with FFR (r = 0.35, p = NS). In contrast, percent increase in 99mTc direct counts with ATP was lower in patients with FFR of < 0.75 than in those with FFR of > = 0.75 (-4 +/- 16% vs 24 +/- 30%, p < 0.01). In addition, a significant correlation (r = 0.70, p < 0.001) was observed between percent increase in 99mTc direct counts with ATP and FFR."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "These results suggest that quantitative analysis of 99mTc-sestamibi scintigraphy enables the assessment of the magnitude of functional significance of coronary stenosis."
        }
      ]},
